selftext:
Ahoy there, I've been working on a 2D fantasy turn-based game for more than half a year now, and I'm getting close to putting together a demo. I started setting up Steamworks to publish the store page and demo, but before I sent it, I wanted to gather more information about Steam handling AI-generated stuff.

&#x200B;

I only used Midjourney, but I edited about 90% of the generated images in Photoshop, having spent close to 100 hours on them. Nevertheless, some images are straight from Midjourney, so can they detect it even though the overall graphics style is unique and uniform? I asked a few graphic artists I know to check out how they like the style, and they didn't even think it was made with AI.

&#x200B;

Is there anybody here who managed to slip through the Steam Anti-AI check with AI-generated graphical assets? Is it better to first create a page with only handmade assets and wait until they accept the initial review, then start including AI-generated stuff? Or do they only filter apps that have integrated AI stuff or have obvious AI-generated assets, and I shouldn't be worrying?
